WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.205
Lesson one point three.

00:00:02.205 --> 00:00:04.985
Wallets, keys, hardware and multisig.

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:06.690
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.737
set up a wallet structure where one

00:00:08.737 --> 00:00:10.840
single mistake can't lose everything.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.455
Here's the problem this lesson solves.

00:00:14.455 --> 00:00:17.917
Most beginners use one wallet for everything: savings,

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:21.050
experiments, new apps, all in the same place.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.625
That means one bad approval, on one sketchy

00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:26.090
app, can reach every dollar you hold.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.004
This lesson replaces that single point of failure

00:00:29.004 --> 00:00:31.742
with structure, so that a mistake stays small,

00:00:31.742 --> 00:00:34.539
contained to exactly the wallet it happened in,

00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:37.045
instead of spreading to everything you own.

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:39.635
Here's the plan.

00:00:39.635 --> 00:00:42.450
First, the five words this lesson runs on.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.501
Second, compartmentalising, the three-wallet setup

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:47.655
that limits any single mistake.

00:00:47.655 --> 00:00:49.484
Third, multisig, in depth, and

00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:51.790
what it actually protects against.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.095
And finally, your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:55.395 --> 00:00:56.310
Five words.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.070
A private key is the authority to sign;

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.260
whoever holds it controls the funds, full stop.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.627
A seed phrase is 12-24 words that

00:01:05.627 --> 00:01:08.341
recreate your keys, never typed into a

00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:12.470
website, never photographed, never shared, with anyone.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.782
A hardware wallet keeps your keys on

00:01:14.782 --> 00:01:17.718
a separate physical device; signing requires a

00:01:17.718 --> 00:01:20.330
physical confirmation on that device.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.947
A multisig needs M of N signers, say

00:01:22.947 --> 00:01:25.510
2 of 3, to agree before it acts.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.215
And a smart wallet is a contract account that

00:01:28.215 --> 00:01:31.450
can add spending limits, recovery options and batching.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.374
One of these deserves zero

00:01:34.374 --> 00:01:36.719
exceptions, ever: the seed phrase.

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:40.039
No legitimate support team, no real person, nothing

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:42.593
legitimate will ever ask you to type it

00:01:42.593 --> 00:01:45.574
anywhere, photograph it, or read it aloud.

00:01:45.574 --> 00:01:47.719
If something asks, it has already told you

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:49.564
everything you need to know about it.

00:01:50.864 --> 00:01:53.479
Smart wallets deserve one more mention here.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:56.613
Because they're contract accounts rather than plain

00:01:56.613 --> 00:01:59.085
key pairs, they can add spending limits,

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:01.496
social recovery if you lose access, and

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:04.749
batching several actions into a single signature.

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:07.197
All of it covered properly in Lesson

00:02:07.197 --> 00:02:10.109
1.9, smart accounts and account abstraction.

00:02:11.409 --> 00:02:12.589
Compartmentalise.

00:02:13.889 --> 00:02:15.309
Here's the structure.

00:02:15.309 --> 00:02:18.581
A vault holds your long-term holdings; it almost

00:02:18.581 --> 00:02:21.585
never signs, and grants no DeFi approvals at

00:02:21.585 --> 00:02:24.289
all, kept on hardware or a multisig.

00:02:24.289 --> 00:02:28.112
An operator holds your active DeFi positions, signing

00:02:28.112 --> 00:02:32.234
only with known, verified protocols, also on hardware.

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:36.150
And a burner holds small amounts for anything experimental,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.779
new apps, mints, airdrops, on a regular hot wallet.

00:02:39.779 --> 00:02:41.780
Money moves down the chain only as

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.724
needed, and profits sweep back up.

00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:47.109
Notice the direction of movement.

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:49.791
Funds flow down, vault to operator to burner,

00:02:49.791 --> 00:02:52.364
only when you actually need them there.

00:02:52.364 --> 00:02:55.224
Profits sweep back up, the opposite direction.

00:02:55.224 --> 00:02:58.196
If the burner gets drained completely, and it might,

00:02:58.196 --> 00:03:01.112
you've lost only the burner's balance, nothing else.

00:03:02.412 --> 00:03:05.442
Here's what that structure actually protects, made

00:03:05.442 --> 00:03:09.307
concrete with an illustrative ten-thousand-dollar portfolio.

00:03:09.307 --> 00:03:11.967
If the burner is drained, you lose about five

00:03:11.967 --> 00:03:15.794
hundred dollars, the small amount kept there for experiments.

00:03:15.794 --> 00:03:18.239
If the operator is compromised, you lose

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:21.759
roughly fifteen hundred, still painful, but contained.

00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:24.837
The vault, holding the other eight thousand, never

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:27.130
touches a DeFi approval at all, so it

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.664
simply isn't exposed to this kind of loss.

00:03:30.964 --> 00:03:32.969
Notice what makes a hardware wallet

00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:35.329
different from a purely software one.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:37.626
A software wallet signs the moment your

00:03:37.626 --> 00:03:40.109
device tells it to, no separate check.

00:03:40.109 --> 00:03:43.335
A hardware wallet requires a physical confirmation, a

00:03:43.335 --> 00:03:46.142
button press on a separate screen, showing you

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:48.532
exactly what you're about to sign, on a

00:03:48.532 --> 00:03:51.160
device malware on your computer can't touch.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.320
One clarification worth making explicit.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.884
These need to be genuinely separate wallets,

00:03:57.884 --> 00:04:00.561
with separate seed phrases, not just different

00:04:00.561 --> 00:04:02.855
accounts derived from the same one.

00:04:02.855 --> 00:04:05.729
Accounts sharing a seed phrase share its fate

00:04:05.729 --> 00:04:08.853
entirely; if that seed is ever compromised, every

00:04:08.853 --> 00:04:11.790
account under it falls at once, vault included.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.481
Losing the hardware device itself isn't

00:04:15.481 --> 00:04:17.455
the disaster it sounds like.

00:04:17.455 --> 00:04:20.290
The device only stores your keys; your seed

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.326
phrase recreates them, identically, on a brand-new device.

00:04:24.326 --> 00:04:27.338
That's exactly why the seed phrase, not the device,

00:04:27.338 --> 00:04:30.177
is the thing worth two separate physical backups.

00:04:31.477 --> 00:04:36.347
This is Lesson 1.1's self-custody property, applied deliberately.

00:04:36.347 --> 00:04:38.972
Nobody can freeze any of these three wallets.

00:04:38.972 --> 00:04:41.934
But equally, nobody recovers them for you either,

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:44.540
which is exactly why the structure, and the

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.672
backups behind it, matter this much.

00:04:47.972 --> 00:04:49.397
Multisig, in depth.

00:04:50.697 --> 00:04:54.364
Here's roughly how two-of-three multisig actually works.

00:04:54.364 --> 00:04:56.448
One signer proposes a transaction,

00:04:56.448 --> 00:04:58.654
spending from the shared wallet.

00:04:58.654 --> 00:05:02.175
A second signer independently reviews it, and approves.

00:05:02.175 --> 00:05:04.206
Only once two signatures agree

00:05:04.206 --> 00:05:06.995
does the transaction actually execute.

00:05:06.995 --> 00:05:09.162
If one key is ever lost or stolen,

00:05:09.162 --> 00:05:11.825
on its own, it simply cannot move anything.

00:05:13.126 --> 00:05:16.317
That's precisely what multisig protects against:

00:05:16.317 --> 00:05:18.766
a single lost or compromised key.

00:05:18.766 --> 00:05:21.148
With two of three required, an attacker

00:05:21.148 --> 00:05:23.828
needs two separate compromises, not one, and

00:05:23.828 --> 00:05:26.270
losing one key yourself still leaves the

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.891
wallet fully recoverable with the other two.

00:05:30.191 --> 00:05:33.091
None of this means you need a multisig today.

00:05:33.091 --> 00:05:36.059
For genuinely small amounts, a single hardware

00:05:36.059 --> 00:05:39.326
wallet, used carefully, is entirely reasonable.

00:05:39.326 --> 00:05:43.065
Multisig earns its added complexity once the amount would

00:05:43.065 --> 00:05:46.223
meaningfully hurt to lose, or once more than one

00:05:46.223 --> 00:05:48.286
person needs a say in moving it.

00:05:49.586 --> 00:05:50.651
A worked example.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:55.004
Maria buys a hardware wallet directly from

00:05:55.004 --> 00:05:58.910
the manufacturer, never a marketplace listing, exactly

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:01.056
the habit from Lesson 0.5.

00:06:01.056 --> 00:06:03.935
She sets up her vault first, moving her long-term

00:06:03.935 --> 00:06:07.171
holdings across, and approving nothing on it, ever.

00:06:07.171 --> 00:06:09.336
She sets up her operator next, a

00:06:09.336 --> 00:06:12.289
second account on that same device, reserved

00:06:12.289 --> 00:06:14.886
for known, verified protocols only.

00:06:14.886 --> 00:06:17.266
And she sets up a burner, a free hot

00:06:17.266 --> 00:06:21.191
wallet, for anything experimental: new apps, mints, airdrops.

00:06:22.491 --> 00:06:24.504
A new app Maria tries turns out

00:06:24.504 --> 00:06:26.328
to be a scam, and drains the

00:06:26.328 --> 00:06:29.081
burner completely: fifty dollars, gone.

00:06:29.081 --> 00:06:32.460
Her vault and operator, sitting in entirely separate

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:36.586
wallets on entirely separate keys, are completely untouched.

00:06:36.586 --> 00:06:40.726
That's compartmentalisation working exactly as designed, not luck.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:45.564
If Maria later wanted a multisig vault instead, setting

00:06:45.564 --> 00:06:49.039
it up usually means a dedicated multisig tool, holding

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:52.182
two or three hardware wallets as the signers.

00:06:52.182 --> 00:06:55.521
Those can belong to different trusted people entirely;

00:06:55.521 --> 00:06:57.827
none of them needs to fully trust the

00:06:57.827 --> 00:07:00.677
others, only the two-of-three rule itself.

00:07:00.677 --> 00:07:03.365
And one more forward reference: keeping any of

00:07:03.365 --> 00:07:06.582
this safe also means keeping it physically private.

00:07:06.582 --> 00:07:08.670
A hardware wallet is only ever as

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.812
safe as the person holding it.

00:07:10.812 --> 00:07:14.342
Lesson 1.8 covers physical security properly.

00:07:15.642 --> 00:07:16.822
Checklist and quiz.

00:07:18.122 --> 00:07:20.751
One detail on that first checklist item:

00:07:20.751 --> 00:07:23.894
two separate physical places means exactly that,

00:07:23.894 --> 00:07:26.374
not two drawers in the same house.

00:07:26.374 --> 00:07:29.571
A fireproof safe and a trusted relative's home,

00:07:29.571 --> 00:07:33.035
or two safety deposit boxes in different locations,

00:07:33.035 --> 00:07:36.299
is the kind of separation that actually survives

00:07:36.299 --> 00:07:38.364
a fire, a flood, or a burglary.

00:07:39.664 --> 00:07:41.559
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:07:41.559 --> 00:07:43.780
Your seed phrase is stored offline,

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.054
in two separate physical places.

00:07:46.054 --> 00:07:47.783
Your vault wallet has never

00:07:47.783 --> 00:07:50.059
approved a single DeFi contract.

00:07:50.059 --> 00:07:52.724
You keep a separate burner wallet for anything new.

00:07:52.724 --> 00:07:55.505
And you read every signing prompt on the hardware

00:07:55.505 --> 00:07:58.119
device's own screen, never just on the website.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:00.629
Question one.

00:08:00.629 --> 00:08:03.434
A 'support agent' in your D.M.s needs your

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:06.279
seed phrase to fix a stuck transaction.

00:08:06.279 --> 00:08:07.129
What do you do?

00:08:11.549 --> 00:08:13.284
The answer: nothing.

00:08:13.284 --> 00:08:16.592
It's a scam, every single time, no exceptions.

00:08:16.592 --> 00:08:17.792
Block and report.

00:08:19.092 --> 00:08:20.337
Question two.

00:08:20.337 --> 00:08:23.112
Why keep the vault wallet completely free of approvals?

00:08:27.532 --> 00:08:31.072
The answer: approvals let contracts move your tokens.

00:08:31.072 --> 00:08:34.150
With none granted, a compromised protocol simply

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.595
has no way to reach it.

00:08:36.895 --> 00:08:38.165
Question three.

00:08:38.165 --> 00:08:41.280
What does two-of-three multisig actually protect against?

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.890
The answer: a single lost or compromised key.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.700
Two signers are still required to move any funds.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.255
Question four.

00:08:54.255 --> 00:08:56.291
Your burner wallet gets completely

00:08:56.291 --> 00:08:58.219
drained by a malicious mint.

00:08:58.219 --> 00:08:59.624
What happens to your vault?

00:09:04.044 --> 00:09:05.779
The answer: nothing.

00:09:05.779 --> 00:09:08.075
It's a separate wallet, holding separate

00:09:08.075 --> 00:09:10.259
funds; the compromise never reaches it.

00:09:11.559 --> 00:09:12.874
Question five.

00:09:12.874 --> 00:09:14.812
What's the one thing no legitimate

00:09:14.812 --> 00:09:16.529
support team will ever ask for?

00:09:20.949 --> 00:09:23.034
The answer: your seed phrase.

00:09:23.034 --> 00:09:25.159
Not once, not for any reason, ever.

00:09:26.459 --> 00:09:29.269
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:29.269 --> 00:09:31.694
Keep your seed phrase offline, always.

00:09:31.694 --> 00:09:34.314
Keep your vault free of approvals, permanently.

00:09:34.314 --> 00:09:38.024
Run active DeFi from an operator wallet, on hardware.

00:09:38.024 --> 00:09:41.249
Use a burner for anything new or experimental.

00:09:41.249 --> 00:09:44.729
And once the amount really matters, move to multisig.

00:09:44.729 --> 00:09:46.416
Do those five things, and one

00:09:46.416 --> 00:09:48.384
mistake can never reach everything.

00:09:49.684 --> 00:09:52.686
This is education, not financial advice, and

00:09:52.686 --> 00:09:56.021
the ten-thousand-dollar example in this lesson is

00:09:56.021 --> 00:09:59.189
illustrative, not a recommended allocation.

00:09:59.189 --> 00:10:01.871
Nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:10:01.871 --> 00:10:04.928
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:10:06.228 --> 00:10:09.689
That's wallets, keys, hardware and multisig: five words,

00:10:09.689 --> 00:10:13.632
the three-wallet setup, and how two-of-three actually works.

00:10:13.632 --> 00:10:17.877
Next, Lesson one point four: tokens, approvals and allowances.
